# Build context for the round keeper is er-demo/ (see Dockerfile).
#
# This file is belt and braces, NOT the mechanism. The Dockerfile copies an explicit list —
# package.json, bun.lock, src/, scripts/, public/idl/ — and never `COPY . .`, so nothing here can
# reach a layer by accident regardless of what this file says. What this file actually buys:
#   1. Context size. 585MB of node_modules and 14MB of design/ never leave the laptop, and the
#      context goes from ~605MB to ~2.6MB.
#   2. A guarantee that key material is not even PRESENT for a future careless COPY to pick up.
# Rule 2 is the one worth keeping when someone later "simplifies" the Dockerfile.
#
# Docker pattern semantics are NOT gitignore semantics: patterns are matched in order and the LAST
# match wins, so `!` re-includes work even under an excluded parent. Where the two agree is the
# `dir/*` + `!dir/sub` form, so that is the form used below — it reads the same to a human coming
# from either file.
#
# Comments must be on their own line. A trailing `#` after a pattern is part of the pattern, not a
# comment, which is a quiet way to build an ignore rule that matches nothing.

# --- Key material. None of this is ever in an image. -------------------------------------------
# .devnet/ holds the live devnet secret keys — the fork payer (arena authority) and the six house
# wallets. It lives at the REPO ROOT, so with the context set to er-demo/ it is already unreachable.
# It is listed anyway because "unreachable" is a property of the build context, not of the repo: the
# day somebody moves the context to the root to share something between engine/ and er-demo/, this
# line is the thing standing between the keys and a published image.
.devnet/
*.key
*.pem
# Matches .env, .env.local and friends, and also .envrc — the direnv shim, which is machine-specific
# and meaningless inside a container.
.env*

# --- Enormous, and reconstructed or unused inside the image ------------------------------------
# ~585MB, reinstalled in the image from bun.lock. Shipping the host's copy would also drop
# darwin-arm64 native binaries into a linux image, where they fail at import time rather than build
# time.
node_modules
# 3.0MB Vite build of the BROWSER app. The keeper has no build step and serves no pages.
dist/
# 14MB of screenshot galleries.
design/
# Rust build output. A repo-root concern, listed for the same reason as .devnet/.
target/
.anchor/
.git
coverage
*.log
.DS_Store
.direnv
.vercel
.idea
.vscode

# --- The other lockfiles ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# bun.lock is the one the build uses and is committed on purpose (er-demo/.gitignore argues why).
# The rest are gitignored already; excluding them means a stray local copy can never leave
# `bun install` with an opinion about which lockfile is authoritative.
bun.lockb
package-lock.json
pnpm-lock.yaml

# --- public/ is browser assets, except the one file the keeper reads off disk -------------------
# src/chain/idl.ts falls back to reading public/idl/bulls_arena.json from disk whenever there is no
# `window`, so the keeper cannot boot without it. Everything else under public/ belongs to the page,
# and public/keeper-status.json is generated by this very process — its ABSENCE is meaningful (a 404
# is correctly read as "no keeper is running here"), so a baked-in copy would be a permanently stale
# heartbeat that reads as a live keeper.
#
# If a Docker version ever parsed this re-include differently, the failure is harmless: a few extra
# KB of favicon and token art would sit in the context and still never be copied, because the
# Dockerfile names public/idl/ explicitly. The exclusion is about size here, not about safety.
public/*
!public/idl

# --- Considered and deliberately NOT excluded ---------------------------------------------------
# *.test.ts — a few KB, and dropping them would make the deployed source tree differ from the repo
# for no measurable gain.
# *.html, tsconfig*.json, vite.config.ts, flake.nix, shell.nix, justfile — browser and toolchain
# files the keeper never reads. They total well under 100KB and the Dockerfile does not copy them,
# so excluding them would be tidying an ignore file rather than changing an image.
